# Watch API — implementation request

**Status: `GET /api/watch/status` is implemented and live** (see
`server/watch.js`, merged in `1866ea7`). The sections below are the
original request, kept for reference. See **Follow-up: day-spanning
history** at the bottom for a new, smaller ask on top of it.

A companion Garmin Connect IQ watch app ("Home Power", repo
`h0me-p0wer-garmin`) polls this backend every 15s for a flat status
JSON. That endpoint does not exist yet in this backend — this file is
the spec for implementing it.

## What's needed

`GET /api/watch/status` — no auth (matches every other endpoint in this
backend), returns the flat shape below directly (NOT wrapped in the
`{ ok, data }` envelope the other endpoints use — the watch app expects
a bare object).

```json
{
  "solar": 4.21,
  "home": 1.87,
  "grid": -2.34,
  "battery": 78,
  "batteryPower": 1.20,
  "solarToday": 18.4,
  "homeToday": 22.6,
  "exportedToday": 18.1,
  "importedToday": 6.4,
  "batteryCapacity": 14.2,
  "batteryMinutesToFull": 195,
  "solarHistory": [0.4, 0.7, 1.1, 1.8, 2.6, 3.4, 4.0, 4.4],
  "homeHistory": [1.2, 1.6, 1.3, 1.1, 1.0, 1.2, 1.4, 1.7],
  "gridHistory": [-0.8, -1.1, -1.6, -2.1, -2.4, -2.8, -3.1, -3.0]
}
```

Every field is optional on the watch side (it merges onto cached
defaults), so partial data beats no endpoint. `grid`: negative =
exporting, positive = importing (same sign convention `/api/flow`
already uses — do not flip it). `batteryPower`: positive = charging.
History arrays are display-only sparklines — any reasonable number of
recent points works; the watch just bar-charts whatever length you
send.

## Where the data already lives

Nothing needs recomputing from scratch — every field has a close
match already flowing through this backend:

- **solar / home / grid / battery / batteryPower** — `computeFlowPayload()`
  in `server/index.js` (backs `GET /api/flow`): `pv.production` (W),
  `home.consumption` (W), `grid.import`/`grid.export` (W, pick sign
  per the convention above), `battery.soc` (%), `battery.discharge` /
  `battery.charge` (W). Convert W→kW (÷1000) for solar/home/grid/
  batteryPower; battery/soc is already a percentage.

- **solarToday / homeToday / exportedToday / importedToday** —
  `GET /api/stats/overview` (`server/stats.js`,
  `registerStatsRoute`) → `data.flows.pvKwh`, `data.flows.homeKwh`,
  `data.flows.gridExportKwh`, `data.flows.gridImportKwh`. (Note:
  `pvKwh` there is PV-direct-to-home, not total production — see the
  comment above `byPeriod.today.pvProducedKwh` in stats.js if total
  production is wanted instead.)

- **batteryCapacity** — `systemCapacityKwh(b)` in `server/index.js`,
  same helper `/api/flow`'s `battery.capacityKwh` already uses.

- **batteryMinutesToFull** — not currently exposed by any endpoint;
  it's computed client-side in the React frontend from soc, capacity,
  charge rate, and the account's charge-ceiling % (see
  `battery.maxPct` / `battery.floorPct` in `/api/flow`, and
  `getBatteryLimits()` in `server/index.js`). This new endpoint should
  do that same arithmetic server-side: minutes = (((maxPct - soc) / 100)
  * capacityKwh) / chargeW * 60, clamped to null/0 when not charging or
  already at ceiling.

- **solarHistory / homeHistory / gridHistory** — `GET /api/timeseries`
  or `GET /api/history` (`server/index.js`) already return time-series
  samples with power values; bucket/sample down to ~8-16 points and
  convert W→kW for each array.

## Suggested approach

A thin new route (in `server/index.js`, alongside the other
`app.get("/api/...")` routes, or a new `server/watch.js` following the
self-registering-module pattern `stats.js`/`roi.js`/`battery-params.js`
use) that calls the existing internal functions above (`computeFlowPayload()`,
the stats-overview logic, `systemCapacityKwh()`) and reshapes their
output into the flat contract. No new data collection, storage, or
polling needed — this is purely a response-shaping layer over data the
backend already has in memory.

## Testing

```sh
curl http://localhost:3001/api/watch/status
```

Response should validate against the JSON shape above. The watch
app's own repo (`h0me-p0wer-garmin/README.md`) documents the same
contract from the client side, and `source/Config.mc` there points
`API_URL` at `https://h0me-p0wer.lucianhanga.stream/api/watch/status`
when `USE_DEMO_DATA` is flipped to `false`.

## Follow-up: day-spanning history

**Done — see `ed951c7`.**

The watch app now renders `solarHistory` (and will likely do the same
for `homeHistory`/`gridHistory` next) as a **line graph spanning the
full day on its x-axis** — 0h on the left, "now" wherever the current
clock time lands, rest of the day left blank — rather than a plain
bar sparkline. It plots whichever points it receives stretched evenly
across that 0h-to-now range, under the assumption they're evenly
spaced samples starting at midnight.

That assumption doesn't hold today: `recentHistory()` in
`server/watch.js` hardcodes `HISTORY_HOURS = 8`, so the arrays are
always a trailing 8-hour window, not midnight-to-now. Late in the day
this makes the graph render a declining-only slice (e.g. the actual
live response at ~19:40 was
`[2.27, 2.44, 2.55, 1.83, 1.75, 1.06, 0.59, 0.08, 0.01]` — all
afternoon/evening) stretched across the whole axis as if it covered
the entire day, instead of the real shape (near-zero at midnight,
ramping up at sunrise, peaking midday, tapering at sunset).

**Ask**: change `recentHistory()`'s window from a fixed trailing
`HISTORY_HOURS` to "since local midnight" — i.e. `from` should be
today's `00:00` local time instead of `now - 8h`, so the array's
first point is always hour 0 and the last is always "now," whatever
that elapsed span is (1 bucket if it's just past midnight, up to ~24
if it's 11pm). `HISTORY_BUCKET_MS` (currently 1h) can probably stay
as-is — at ~24 points max that's still well within what `points`-less
`bucket` querying to `/api/timeseries` handles fine, per the existing
comment there about `bucket` vs `points`.

Same applies to `homeHistory`/`gridHistory`, which share the same
`recentHistory()` call — no separate fix needed once the window logic
changes, all three come from the same loop.

## Follow-up 2: PV→battery split for the Solar graph (new field)

**Done — see `7438a37`.** That same change also went further than this
ask: `batteryMinutesToEmpty`/`batteryFloorPct`/`batteryMaxPct`,
`homeFromGridHistory`/`homeFromBatteryHistory` (the Home page's
stacked-bar split), and `solarDirectToday`/`batteryDischargedToday`/
`batteryChargedToday` (`7438a37`, `ff62f32`, fixed in `a2362fc`) are
all live too — the current full contract lives in
`h0me-p0wer-garmin/README.md`, which is the source of truth from here
on; this file stays as a historical record of the original ask.

The Solar page now renders a **stacked area** under the production
line — PV→battery stacked below PV→home, total production traced on
top — the same composition as the web app's own Graph tab "Power
Production" chart (`web/src/graph/GraphTab.jsx`: `pvBatt` + `pvHome`
stacked series, `pv` total line on top, colors `#3DA568` / `#8EE3A8` /
`#5FCE80`).

The watch has `solarHistory` (total) but nothing for the split, so
right now it falls back to a static demo curve for the battery band,
which doesn't match real data and just looks wrong once the real
production line is live.

**Ask**: add a new array field, same cadence/length as `solarHistory`:

```json
"solarBatteryHistory": [0.0, 0.1, 0.4, 0.9, 1.2, 1.1, 0.6, 0.1]
```

kW of that interval's PV production routed to the battery (i.e. the
`pvBatt` series' values, W→kW same as the other history arrays). The
watch computes PV→home itself as `solarHistory[i] - solarBatteryHistory[i]`
per point — no need to send that separately. Likely source: the same
`/api/timeseries` call `recentHistory()` already makes should have (or
can be extended to have) a per-bucket PV→battery figure alongside the
`pv` figure it already reads — check what `computeFlowPayload()`'s
`pvToBattery` derivation (used by `/api/flow`) corresponds to in the
timeseries data, or whether that split needs adding to the timeseries
endpoint's own bucket aggregation.
